{"conditionalSetIds":[],"forecasterId":"demographics-ai-2036:run2:inflation:reconciler","informationSetArtifactId":"sha256:12d05d5d5e17481221571fcb9c877e138fefae78205da7c40f240c3c96bd9943","modelForecastIds":[],"modelUse":"none","prediction":{"kind":"binary","positiveOutcomeId":"yes","probability":0.32},"questionHash":"sha256:a11e957a2fbf9e72130705a8815d67b5a6c66c3444a8332ee92cdcb4248068e1","questionId":"demographics-ai-2036-cq2","questionVersion":"1.0.0","rationale":"Wave spread 14/24/32/34/35. I land at 32 and decompose it differently from every panelist who reached a similar number.\nCONJUNCT A (GDP-weighted OECD-38 core CPI/PCE >=2.50%/yr avg 2027-2036) = 0.64. The decisive fact is the one Fermi found and labelled a \"measurement-convention question\" before moving on: the OECD-38 aggregate is not the core-OECD aggregate. Historian's 2.14% composite is a correct answer to a different question. I carry the weights explicitly, and I resolve the convention Fermi declined to resolve — but only halfway, against the Red-Teamer. On MARKET GDP the five high-inflation members (Turkiye, Mexico, Chile, Colombia, Costa Rica) are ~7-8.5% of OECD-38; on the constant-2025-USD-PPP basis Section 3 locks they are ~11% ($8.9T of ~$87T PPP: Turkiye ~3.6, Mexico ~3.3, Colombia ~1.1, Chile ~0.7, Costa Rica ~0.16). The lock's own exception clause is triggered by CQ2's named index (local core CPI/PCE), so whether the WEIGHTS follow PPP is genuinely undetermined by the question text — the Red-Teamer asserts PPP as settled and it is not. I therefore carry ~10% as the expectation over conventions. Bloc rate: PPP-internal weights Turkiye 0.40 / Mexico 0.37 / Colombia 0.12 / Chile 0.08 / CR 0.02 at 12 / 4 / 4.5 / 3.5 / 2.5 gives 7.15%/yr over a decade whose first three years still contain double-digit Turkish inflation. Bloc contribution 0.60-0.80pp; I use 0.70pp. MY BASKET BUILD, carrying the Red-Teamer's imputation correction at HALF strength. He is right that the Wave-1 cold leg is overstated: in core PCE the AI-exposed bloc is dominated by non-market-priced lines — portfolio management and investment advice priced as a share of AUM (which INFLATES in an AI-capex equity boom), financial services imputed through net interest margin (which widens on my CQ4 path), P&C insurance as premiums net of claims, and professional services deflated by cost-based PPI proxies where a labour-input-cost fall is booked as lower real OUTPUT rather than lower price. He is also right on the shelter SIGN: aging RAISES households per capita (widowhood, single occupancy, aging-in-place suppressing turnover and therefore effective supply) — Japan's household count rose for roughly two decades past its population peak — so Historian's use of suppressed household formation to hold shelter down is wrong-signed. But he over-applies both at once and ignores a third weight that cuts the other way: euro-area HICP core carries only ~7% rent, because owner-occupied housing is excluded, so the euro leg is structurally low-beta to exactly the shelter channel he is levering, and the imputed financial lines are an ~0.08 weight in core PCE, not a decisive bloc. US core CPI convention: shelter 0.44x3.2 = 1.41 + medical/care 0.11x3.3 = 0.36 + other services 0.24x2.5 = 0.60 + core goods 0.21x0.5 = 0.105 = 2.48%. US core PCE convention: shelter 0.18x3.2 = 0.58 + health 0.17x2.9 = 0.49 + financial/insurance 0.08x2.8 = 0.22 + other services 0.33x2.4 = 0.79 + core goods 0.24x0.4 = 0.10 = 2.18%. Blend (core CPI has historically run ~0.3pp above core PCE) -> US 2.40%. PPP-weighted OECD-38: US 0.344x2.40 = 0.826; euro-area members 0.258x2.10 = 0.542; Japan 0.079x1.80 = 0.142; UK 0.050x2.60 = 0.130; Korea 0.039x2.00 = 0.078; Canada 0.031x2.30 = 0.071; Australia 0.022x2.60 = 0.057; the five high-inflation members 0.102x7.00 = 0.714; Poland 0.022x3.00 = 0.066; remainder 0.053x2.40 = 0.127. SUM = 2.65%. Time profile: 2027-2030 ~2.85%, 2031-2036 ~2.47% (the impulses — grid/power capex crowding, business-electricity pass-through into services input costs, the tariff/fragmentation LEVEL shift, the first care-wage wave — are front-loaded and partly one-off; the EM contribution decays only as Turkiye disinflates, which is slow). Sigma on a 10-year average ~0.38. P(>=2.50) = 1 - Phi((2.50-2.65)/0.38) = 0.65; I shade to 0.64 for the Bayesian's correlated-evidence discipline, which is the reason I am not at the Red-Teamer's 0.72: he multiplies three one-directional corrections (full imputation, shelter 3.5, PPP EM weights) as if independent, which is precisely the failure the Bayesian avoided by collapsing four hot core prints into one LR-2.2 cluster instead of a product of ~4.5.\nCONJUNCT B (China headline CPI <1.50%/yr avg 2027-2036) = 0.47, below the Wave-1 centre. The Bayesian landed the cleanest kill in Wave 2 on the Scout's \"single most under-priced signal\": China ran PPI ~+6.3% in 2017 with CPI +1.6% and ~+8.1% in 2021 with CPI +0.9%, so P(PPI spike | CPI stays low) is HIGH and the PPI sign flip carries an LR near 0.75-0.85, not the 0.4-0.5 its framing implies — before the Scout's own caveat that the +5.0% September 2026 print is single-source and unconfirmed (\"I couldn't find any source reporting China's September 2026 PPI at +5%, so I can't confirm that figure\"). I therefore do NOT take B down on PPI. I take it down on two structural points the Red-Teamer made and no one answered: the property drag is a STOCK adjustment with an end (38 months of falling new-home prices, housing share of household assets 67% -> 52%, modal completion 2029-31, leaving five years on the other side of it), and the locked 2A arithmetic FLIPS the labour sign in the back half (-60M population 2026-35, worker/retiree 4.4 -> 2.8, so service-sector unit labour costs turn up through the 2030s), against a Japan analogue that deflated with a chronically strong yen and no industrial-policy price floor where China runs a managed-depreciating currency, reflationary for headline. The decade bar is tight: four years at the observed ~0.9-1.2% leaves the remaining six needing under ~1.7%, and Fermi's own split window (4x0.8 + 6x1.9)/10 = 1.46% is knife-edge — a back half at 2.2% instead of 1.9% gives 1.64% and B FAILS. I discount the Red-Teamer's hog-cycle/oil argument: a one-year 2pp spike moves a decade mean by ~0.2pp, which is material but not the 0.42-level driver he treats it as. 0.47.\nCORRELATION +0.08. Three of five lenses took it negative on the export-deflation channel (a 3%/yr China export price fall at 30% pass-through into a ~21% core-goods weight is -0.19pp/yr on OECD core). That channel is real but it is the lightest and most tariff-walled leg of core. The Scout named and then nearly zeroed the dominant channel, which the Red-Teamer correctly promoted: tariffs and fragmentation SIMULTANEOUSLY raise OECD goods prices and glut China's domestic market with diverted export supply, which produces exactly the joint cell asked for and is the regime 2A describes. The wall is leaky and a common BIS-Box-C demand shortfall drags both down together. Small net positive, not the Red-Teamer's +0.20.\nJOINT = 0.64 x 0.47 + 0.08 x sqrt(0.64x0.36x0.47x0.53) = 0.3008 + 0.08 x 0.2396 = 0.320 -> 32.","referenceClassIds":[],"schemaVersion":"forecast-workbench.forecast/v1","sealedAt":"2026-11-04T17:31:57.247Z","seriesId":"published-panel","strongestContraryCase":"Not elicited per question. Panelist falsification signposts: {\"signpost\":\"Turkiye annual CPI (TurkStat, December print) averages below 8.0%/yr over calendar 2028-2031 AND Mexico's headline CPI (INEGI) averages below 3.5% over the same four years. Both required, so a single good Turkish year does not trigger it.\",\"by_when\":\"Feb 28, 2032 (full-year 2031 releases)\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the single load-bearing term in my CQ2: the five high-inflation OECD-38 members contributing ~0.70pp to GDP-weighted core. That contribution would fall toward ~0.40pp and my 2027-2036 aggregate from 2.65% to ~2.35%. CQ2 conjunct A falls from 0.64 to ~0.42 and CQ2 from 32 to ~21; CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~16 and CQ6-D rises from 13 to ~18, with B absorbing the balance; CQ4 P50 falls ~0.15pp to ~2.05; hypothesis plank (c) rises from 45 to ~58. My whole divergence from the Historian's CQ2 of 14 would stand revealed as a weighting convention rather than a finding.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"BLS PPI for software publishers AND for computer systems design, plus the euro-area HICP professional-services sub-indices, show OUTRIGHT annual declines of 1.0% or more for two consecutive years in those market-priced (not cost- or asset-fee-imputed) series.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the half of the Red-Teamer's imputation attack that I accepted — that the genuinely deflatable slice of core is only 0.08-0.12 rather than 0.20-0.25 and is partly counter-cyclically hot. My 2032-36 core central falls from 2.45% to ~2.25%, taking P(core >= 2.70%) from 0.27 to ~0.14: CQ6-C falls from 22 to ~14, B rises to ~56, D rises to ~15, A unchanged at 15; CQ2 falls to ~25. The Wave-1 unanimous 'extreme skew, dull aggregate' thesis would be vindicated against both me and the Red Team, and he would be the effort's worst input rather than its best corrective.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"China headline CPI (NBS), three-year average over calendar 2031-2033, exceeds 2.2%/yr, with the services sub-index above the goods sub-index in at least two of those three years. My split-window build is 2027-2030 at ~1.0% and 2031-2036 at ~1.9-2.1%, giving a decade average knife-edge against the 1.50% bar.\",\"by_when\":\"Mar 31, 2034\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Collapses CQ2's China conjunct from 0.47 to ~0.18, taking CQ2 from 32 to ~17. It also means the Chinese labour-scarcity services-wage channel turned reflationary earlier than the locked 2A arithmetic implies, which raises China's growth leg: CQ3 P50 rises ~0.10pp to ~1.85 and P10 to ~1.0, CQ6-D falls ~3 to 10 and A rises ~2 to 17. The services-above-goods condition is there so that a hog cycle or an oil shock alone does not fire it.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"Count of OECD-38 members that enact a binding national or sector-wide care-wage award worth 5% or more in real terms, cumulatively over 2027-2031 (tracked in OECD Health at a Glance and national LTC reimbursement schedules), reaches six or more — against zero or one as the null.\",\"by_when\":\"Dec 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"This is the cheapest available test of the mechanism on which I overrode the Bayesian Updater's CQ1 of 8, namely that administered care prices move in discrete statutory level jumps rather than never. Six or more awards takes CQ1's second conjunct from 0.22 to ~0.33 and CQ1 from 16 to ~24, raises plank (a) from 6 to ~12, and adds ~0.08pp to my core path via the ISIC Q channel (CQ2 to ~34, CQ6-C to ~24). Zero or one takes conjunct B to ~0.12 and CQ1 to ~10, i.e. all the way back to the Wave-1 centre, and vindicates the pure fiscal-quantity-rationing read.\"} | {\"signpost\":\"ICE BofA MOVE calendar-year average stays below 85 in every year 2027-2031 while the G7 GDP-weighted 10y nominal sovereign yield averages above 4.0% across the same five years. Both required: a low-vol, high-rate regime is the configuration that kills the level-scaling term.\",\"by_when\":\"Jan 31, 2032\",\"if_observed_implies\":\"Falsifies the baseline assumption behind my CQ5 conjunct A of 0.37 — that a ~85-95 structural baseline plus one 12-18 month crisis clears a decade mean of 105 — and vindicates the Scout's reading that the 2026 compression at 64-71 with nominals already at 3-4% is structural rather than policy-certainty-driven. CQ5 falls from 27 to ~15; CQ4 P90 falls from 3.80 to ~3.30 and P50 to ~2.05 as the term-premium add is shown to be unwarranted; CQ6-C falls ~4 to 18 with B absorbing it. The reverse — MOVE averaging above 110 in both 2029 and 2030 — takes CQ5 to ~38 and CQ4 P50 to ~2.50.\"}","updateBasis":{"kind":"launch","note